    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the meaning is that of section 105(1) of the Children Act 1989, which is not among the sources

-- (1) "'employer', in relation to a person who is an employee, means a person who (first limb) under section 6 is liable to
-- pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of the earnings of the person who is an employee, or (second limb)
-- would be liable to pay such contributions but for (A) the condition in section 6(1)(b), or (B) the employee being under
-- the age of 16."  The limb labels are not in the converter text; "first limb", "second limb", "(A)" and "(B)" are mine.
-- src:3976 | “employer”, in relation to a person who is an employee, means a person who—
-- src:3977 | under section 6 is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of the earnings of the person who is an employee, or
-- src:3978 | would be liable to pay such contributions but for—
-- src:3979 | the condition in section 6(1)(b), or
-- src:3980 | the employee being under the age of 16;
@ref s 171ZZ4(1) (src:3976-3980)
GIVEN `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to earnings of the employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 6 the person would be liable but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 6 the person would be liable but for the employee being under 16` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 173(a) the person is under 16` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZZ4(1) the person is the employer of the employee` MEANS
    IF     `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to earnings of the employee`
        OR `s 6 the person would be liable but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF NOT `s 6 the person would be liable but for the employee being under 16`
         THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employer”, in relation to a person who is an employee, means a person who" is liable, or would be but for the condition or the age
         ELSE CONSIDER `s 173(a) the person is under 16`
              WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): s 173(a) does not answer whether the employee is under 16
              WHEN JUST under16 THEN
                  IF under16 THEN JUST TRUE
                  ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "means a person who" would be liable "but for ... the employee being under the age of 16"; the employee is not under 16

-- Nouns for the kinds of change that "modifications" names.
DECLARE `s 171ZZ4(1) kind of change` IS ONE OF
    `s 171ZZ4(1) an addition`
    `s 171ZZ4(1) an omission`
    `s 171ZZ4(1) an amendment`
    `s 171ZZ4(1) another kind of change`

-- (1) "'modifications' includes additions, omissions and amendments, and related expressions are to be read accordingly".
-- src:3985 | “modifications” includes additions, omissions and amendments, and related expressions are to be read accordingly;
@ref s 171ZZ4(1) (src:3985)
GIVEN kind IS A `s 171ZZ4(1) kind of change`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZZ4(1) the change is a modification` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `s 171ZZ4(1) an addition` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171ZZ4(1) an omission` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171ZZ4(1) an amendment` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171ZZ4(1) another kind of change` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "includes additions, omissions and amendments" does not close the list

-- (1) "'prescribed' means prescribed by regulations".
-- src:3986 | “prescribed” means prescribed by regulations.
@ref s 171ZZ4(1) (src:3986)
GIVEN `the matter is prescribed by regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZZ4(1) the matter is prescribed` MEANS
    IF `the matter is prescribed by regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“prescribed” means prescribed by regulations"

-- (2) "In this Part 'employee' means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in
-- an office (including elective office) with general earnings (as defined by section 7 of the Income Tax (Earnings and Pensions)
-- Act 2003)."  "Gainfully" is read as adding nothing to "with general earnings" (fork in NOTES).  Whether the person has general
-- earnings in that sense is a borrowed fact.  Whether adjacent waters count as Great Britain (s 172(a)) is already in the core's field.
-- src:3987 | (2) In this Part “employee” means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with general earnings (as defined by section 7 of the Income Tax (Earnings and Pensions) Act 2003).
@ref s 171ZZ4(2) (src:3987)
GIVEN employment IS AN Employment
      `the person has general earnings as defined by section 7 of the Income Tax (Earnings and Pensions) Act 2003` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZZ4(2) the person is an employee` MEANS
    IF     employment's `carried on in Great Britain`
       AND (employment's `held under a contract of service` OR employment's `held in an office`)
       AND `the person has general earnings as defined by section 7 of the Income Tax (Earnings and Pensions) Act 2003`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with general earnings"

-- (3) and (4): powers to treat a person as, or not as, an employee and to treat employers or contracts as one: inert, machinery.

-- (5) "In this Part, except where otherwise provided, 'week' means a period of seven days beginning with Sunday or such other period as
-- may be prescribed in relation to any particular case or class of cases."  The core's fifth week convention.
-- src:3994 | (5) In this Part, except where otherwise provided, “week” means a period of seven days beginning with Sunday or such other period as may be prescribed in relation to any particular case or class of cases.
@ref s 171ZZ4(5) (src:3994)
`s 171ZZ4(5) the week convention` MEANS `a period of 7 days beginning with Sunday or another prescribed period`

-- src:3994 | (5) In this Part, except where otherwise provided, “week” means a period of seven days beginning with Sunday or such other period as may be prescribed in relation to any particular case or class of cases.
@ref s 171ZZ4(5) (src:3994)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `regulations prescribe another period as the week in the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 171ZZ4(5) the first day of the week containing the day` MEANS
    IF `regulations prescribe another period as the week in the case`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "such other period as may be prescribed"
    ELSE JUST (Date (Day day MINUS (`Weekday of` day)))

-- (6) "a person's normal weekly earnings are, subject to subsection (8), to be taken to be the average weekly earnings which
-- in the relevant period have been paid to the person or paid for the person's benefit under the contract of service with
-- the employer in question."  (7): "earnings" and "relevant period" are whatever regulations say, so the total and the
-- number of weeks are inputs.  (8): in prescribed cases regulations calculate it, so the text alone cannot answer.
-- src:3995 | (6) For the purposes of this Part, a person's normal weekly earnings are, subject to subsection (8), to be taken to be the average weekly earnings which in the relevant period have been paid to the person or paid for the person's benefit under the contract of service with the employer in question.
-- src:3996 | (7) For the purposes of subsection (6) “earnings” and “relevant period” have the meanings given to them by regulations.
-- src:3997 | (8) In such cases as may be prescribed, a person's normal weekly earnings are to be calculated in accordance with regulations.
@ref s 171ZZ4(6) (src:3995-3997)
GIVEN `the earnings paid in the relevant period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of weeks in the relevant period` IS A NUMBER
      `regulations prescribe another calculation in the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 171ZZ4(6) the person's normal weekly earnings in pounds` MEANS
    IF `regulations prescribe another calculation in the case`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): subsection (8), "In such cases as may be prescribed"
    ELSE IF `the number of weeks in the relevant period` GREATER THAN 0
         THEN JUST (`the earnings paid in the relevant period, in pounds` DIVIDED BY `the number of weeks in the relevant period`)
         ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): an average over a relevant period with no weeks is not defined

-- (9) to (12): the power to let a person elect to treat contracts divided by NHS reorganisation as one contract, what the regulations
-- may prescribe, the powers' independence, and the concurrence of the Commissioners: inert, machinery.

§§ `s 171ZZ5 Power to apply Part 12ZC`

-- (1) to (3): powers to apply this Part, with modifications, to adoption cases that are not placements under UK law and to
-- parental order cases, and to impose statutory declarations: inert, machinery.  Nothing here for the person until regulations are made.
